  "question": "I want to compute brightness temperature vs. snow depth as a function of wetness.",
  "answer": "**Conclusion:** Snow wetness controls how brightness temperature responds to snow depth by transitioning from scattering-dominated to absorption-dominated emission.\n\n---\n\n### Results from SMRT@1.5.1 [model:smrt@1.5.1]\n\nThree 37 GHz, 55° incidence runs over thickness 0.05–2.0 m, 300 kg m⁻³ density, with a default correlation length of 0.15 mm:\n\n| Wetness | Temp | TB_v (0.05 m → 2.0 m) | TB_h (0.05 m → 2.0 m) | Depth signal |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dry (0% LW)** | 265 K | 259.3 K → 233.0 K | 244.1 K → 213.8 K | Clear decline (–26 K V, –30 K H) |\n| **Wet (0.5% LW)** | 273.15 K | *see chart* | *see chart* | *transition* |\n| **Wet (2% LW)** | 273.15 K | 272.66 K (flat) | 254.53 K (flat) | Fully opaque |\n\n**Chart:** Vertical-polarization brightness temperature vs. snow depth for the three wetness cases.\n\n---\n\n### Physical interpretation\n\n1. **Dry snow (0% liquid water):** Increasing depth increases scattering, reducing the ground contribution and lowering brightness temperature. The slope magnitude depends on grain size; with the default 0.15 mm, TB falls by ~26 K (V) and ~30 K (H) as depth goes from 5 cm to 2 m.\n\n2. **Wet snow (0.5%–2% liquid water):** Liquid water absorption overwhelms scattering. Even 0.5% volumetric liquid water (0.5% by volume) is enough to make the snowpack largely opaque, so TB no longer depends on depth. At 2% LW, TB is constant and equals the snow's physical temperature (273.15 K) in vertical polarization, and the emission is dominated by the snowpack itself rather than the ground.\n\n3. **The transition:** The depth signal is lost once liquid water absorption makes the layer optically thick at the observation frequency. At 37 GHz on rough soil, this occurs between ~0.1% and ~1% volumetric liquid water.\n\n> The default correlation length of 0.15 mm was used; results scale with grain size. For a different grain size (e.g., 0.25 mm for older snow), the depth sensitivity in the dry case would be stronger, and the wetness threshold for opacity would shift slightly.",
